"""Nineteen distinct learning objectives across a furnishing element's lifecycle.
The renderer combines these protocols with the component evidence, not invented product specifications.
"""
STAGES=[]
def stage(slug,title,question,sections,lab,faqs,fields,focus,financial=False):
 STAGES.append(dict(slug=slug,title=title,question=question,sections=sections,lab=lab,faqs=faqs,fields=fields,focus=focus,financial=financial))
stage('design-brief','Write the design brief','What should this element do for the actual stay?',[
('Define the activity before the purchase',
'''A useful brief describes the task an element must support and the conditions in which it will be used. Start with the guest activity, the operating team's responsibilities and the retained property constraints. A product name alone does not explain the required result. Write an acceptance condition that another person can observe, rather than a broad preference such as attractive, premium or guest-friendly.

Distinguish a missing function from a condition issue. If the existing item is damaged, incompatible or difficult to maintain, record that evidence separately. A new visual concept should not hide a problem requiring professional assessment. Keep relevant technical, installation and product questions assigned to the people qualified to answer them.'''),
('Separate requirements, preferences and assumptions',
'''Give each statement a status. A measured opening is a property fact, a proposed finish is a preference and a delivery date without confirmation is an assumption. This makes the brief usable during selection and prevents an attractive presentation from turning an estimate into a commitment. Record the source, date and reviewer for important facts.

Identify who prepares the decision and who approves it. Include what remains outside the assignment, such as specialist work, receiving, care trials or photography. If a design service does not include implementation, the brief needs a named local owner for that work. Do not silently assign it to whichever person is on site later.'''),
('Use one approved brief across the project',
'''Send the same accepted version to the designer, purchaser and local operating team. When a requirement changes, record its reason and the affected products, budget lines and instructions. Retain the earlier version so the decision can be understood later. The installed result must eventually be checked against the final brief, not an obsolete concept board.

The worked record should finish with a specific function, verified constraints, open questions and an approval route. It does not need to predict rental revenue. Its immediate value is that different people can make compatible decisions from the same property information.''')],
'''Make a one-page brief with three columns: required function, supporting evidence and acceptance test. Add a fourth column for the owner of anything unresolved. Review it with the local team before browsing alternatives. Remove a statement that cannot be interpreted consistently, or rewrite it as a testable question. Then compare the brief with the current property inventory and identify what can remain. Save the version used to request quotations and note which later changes would require a new approval.''',
[('Is a style label a complete brief?','No. Style can guide visual relationships, but the brief also needs the actual function, constraints, care and implementation responsibilities. Keep the preference separate from facts that require site evidence.'),('Should the brief include an assumed budget?','It can include a clearly labeled planning allowance. Record its basis and keep unquoted work visible. An allowance is not a vendor commitment or evidence that the project can be completed for that amount.'),('When is the brief ready?','When its intended result, important constraints, unresolved questions and approval responsibility are explicit. Readiness does not require pretending every uncertainty has already been resolved.')],
['Intended guest task','Retained constraints','Required result','Preference versus fact','Open question and verifier','Approved brief revision'],['function','selection','test'])
stage('measurement-survey','Prepare the measurement survey','Which site facts must be verified before a fit-sensitive decision?',[
('Survey the installed context',
'''Measure the element in relation to the actual room and its tasks. Record fixed openings, nearby furniture in use, controls and access points relevant to the decision. A room's overall width and length may not describe the useful space. Show drawers, doors and convertible parts in the positions guests and the operating team will actually use.

Use consistent units, room identifiers and a dated sketch. Pair measurements with labeled photographs so the reader can locate the observation. Record the person who measured and mark approximations. A wide-angle image can support context but should not be treated as proof of a critical dimension.'''),
('Distinguish fit, access and operation',
'''An item can fit its final location while failing its delivery route. It can also pass delivery and still interfere with another task when operated. Keep assembled, packed and moving dimensions separate where the product requires them. Obtain exact supplier information rather than scaling a retail photograph.

Trace relevant unloading, door, stair, lift and turning conditions with the responsible delivery or installation provider. Have qualified professionals interpret building, structural or accessibility requirements where applicable. The survey organizes evidence; it does not certify compliance or prove that an unfamiliar product will pass a difficult route.'''),
('Resolve uncertainties before commitment',
'''Assign remeasurement for dimensions that control a costly or irreversible decision. Record the deadline and which purchase remains on hold. If flooring, trim, equipment or another fixed condition changes, check whether the accepted survey still applies. A superseded drawing should remain available as history but be clearly marked.

Close the survey when the critical facts are supported and remaining uncertainty is visible to the approver. Save the version used for selection and installation. The next person should be able to distinguish an actual measurement from an assumption without reconstructing an email thread.''')],
'''Draw the relevant area twice: ordinary use and the expanded or servicing configuration. Mark the element's actual dimensions, nearby moving parts and any unresolved measurement. Put the packed specification and delivery route on a separate sheet when physical delivery matters. Ask the local verifier to close one critical question with a dated photograph and measurement. Review which selections depend on that answer and preserve the accepted survey with the purchasing record.''',
[('Can dimensions from another unit be reused?','They can inform a question, but they do not verify this property. Record the actual relevant conditions and remeasure fit-sensitive facts before commitment.'),('Does a room measurement prove delivery access?','No. The packed item and route have their own constraints. Obtain supplier information and the relevant provider review instead of treating final-room fit as a delivery guarantee.'),('What if work changes the room?','Recheck the affected dimensions and update the approved survey. Tell the purchaser and installer which version applies before another irreversible decision is made.')],
['Room and survey date','Units and measuring person','Normal configuration','Expanded or service configuration','Critical unverified dimension','Accepted drawing reference'],['measure','test','records'])
stage('product-specification','Build the specification','What exact product or deliverable meets the required function?',[
('Specify the whole element',
'''A specification should identify the actual product or deliverable, not only a visual description. Record model or revision, dimensions, material or construction information, intended use, compatible components and required instructions. For a custom or service deliverable, define format, scope, revision allowance and acceptance evidence instead of inventing a retail model number.

Separate confirmed information from vendor marketing and unresolved questions. Terms such as durable, commercial, turnkey or easy-care do not establish every requirement in the brief. Ask for the documents and scope details that matter to the actual property. Missing information remains an open selection question, not proof that a feature is included or unavailable.'''),
('Review dependencies and serviceability',
'''Check the element's relationships with retained products, local care, installation and access. A visually similar option may change those dependencies. Review exact replacement components, repair support and any limits on intended use. Current availability is useful evidence, but it is not a promise that a matching part will exist indefinitely.

Read the complete care and warranty information relevant to the proposed use. Distinguish the coverage of materials, components, labor and transport where the documents make those distinctions. Obtain qualified review for technical suitability rather than using a broad product label as a substitute.'''),
('Freeze an approved version before ordering',
'''Create a short approved schedule with the identifying information, selection reason and remaining exceptions. Connect it to the current measured plan and required acceptance test. Give the purchaser and receiver enough information to recognize an incorrect finish, revision or quantity.

If the supplier proposes a substitution, reopen the relevant specification checks. Record who approves the change and update all affected instructions. The purpose of an approved specification is consistent implementation, not the appearance of certainty about facts nobody has verified.''')],
'''Put the proposed element beside the design brief in a two-column review. For every requirement, identify the exact product document or written scope that supports it. Mark an unsupported requirement as a question for the provider. Check compatibility with the installed context and the local care process. Finish with an approved identifier and a list of changes that would require another review. Keep that record attached to the order rather than relying on the product image alone.''',
[('Is a product photograph enough?','No. It helps explain appearance, but important dimensions, use requirements, compatibility and care need exact information. Record what the image does and does not establish.'),('Can a similar model use the same approval?','Only after the relevant requirements are compared. Differences in size, construction, care or components can affect the property even when the appearance is close.'),('How much detail belongs in the schedule?','Enough to identify and verify the accepted result and its dependencies. Keep specialist documents linked instead of replacing them with an unverified summary.')],
['Exact identifier or scope','Required function','Confirmed specification','Supporting document','Compatibility question','Approved version and exception'],['selection','measure','records'])
stage('sample-and-use-trial','Run the sample and use trial','Which uncertainty can a controlled ordinary-use review resolve?',[
('Choose a narrow question',
'''A trial should answer a defined question about fit, appearance, care, clarity or ordinary operation. State it before selecting a sample. A positive impression without a question is difficult to compare with another result. Use the actual installed context where practical and identify how a sample differs from the final product.

Follow exact product instructions and obtain permission or qualified support where relevant. Do not invent structural, chemical or electrical tests. The trial is a planning exercise, not certification of safety, lifespan or suitability for every guest. Keep technical concerns with the responsible professional.'''),
('Record conditions and observations',
'''Write the date, location, product revision, light or room conditions and permitted method. Record what happened, not only whether somebody liked it. A care trial may reveal an equipment or drying dependency; a layout trial may expose a door conflict. Those findings call for different remedies.

Compare alternatives using the same defined task when possible. If conditions differ, preserve that fact rather than presenting the results as a controlled experiment. Keep preferences distinct from observed failure. A different guest or operating team may value the same feature differently.'''),
('Decide what the trial supports',
'''Accept, reject or request more evidence for the requirement being tested. Avoid extending one result into a claim about all future use. A successful cleaning of an approved sample does not guarantee that every stain can be removed or that the whole product will last a stated period.

Carry the accepted result into the specification, care record or guest instructions. Identify the changes that would invalidate it, such as a new material, revision or installation. The trial should close a specific uncertainty and leave the remaining ones visible.''')],
'''Write the question, permitted method and evidence to collect. Run the ordinary task with the actual local participant who will use or maintain the element. Photograph the relevant condition and record any change in fit, clarity or appearance. Compare the result with the acceptance condition, then decide what action follows. Keep a limitation statement beside the result so a later purchaser does not mistake a narrow trial for a universal product endorsement.''',
[('Does a successful sample prove product lifespan?','No. It supports the specific observation under recorded conditions. Lifespan, warranty and technical suitability require their own evidence and should not be inferred from a short planning trial.'),('Should every trial include a purchase?','Not necessarily. A measured mock-up, provider demonstration or permitted sample may answer the question. Choose the least consequential method that produces useful evidence.'),('What if the result is unclear?','Keep the question open and identify the next evidence needed. Do not convert uncertainty into approval merely to keep an order moving.')],
['Question being tested','Exact sample or setup','Permitted method','Conditions and participant','Observation versus preference','Decision and limitation'],['test','selection','failure'])
stage('landed-cost-budget','Build the landed-cost budget','What does the complete accepted result cost, and which amounts remain assumptions?',[
('Separate the cost layers',
'''Record the approved quantity and product or service price, then identify the additional work required to make the element usable. Depending on scope, this can include tax, freight, receiving, storage, assembly, mounting, specialist work, cleanup or a return. Do not assume every line applies or invent a charge where the vendor has not quoted one.

Mark each amount as quoted, committed, paid, credited or an allowance. A blank cost is unresolved, not evidence of a free service. Keep currency and quote date visible. Compare totals only after the intended result and responsibilities are the same.'''),
('Show arithmetic and uncertainty separately',
'''Use a transparent line calculation: quantity multiplied by unit cost, plus the applicable quoted charges, less documented discounts or credits. Check whether tax or freight is already included before adding it again. Keep planning reserves distinct from vendor amounts, with their basis stated rather than hidden in every item.

A useful worked calculation can use hypothetical numbers, clearly labeled as arithmetic. It does not establish market pricing or a return on design. Replace the assumptions with actual quotes before approving a purchase. Review cash timing separately from total cost so deposits and outstanding balances remain understandable.'''),
('Reconcile the installed result',
'''At closeout, compare the approved commitment with invoices, payments, refunds, substitutions and accepted quantity. Connect the actual installed element to those records. A paid invoice does not automatically show that delivery, installation and acceptance have occurred.

Keep a useful description of what was purchased and what work was performed. The design budget organizes costs; the accountant determines reporting and tax treatment from supported facts. Where a property study is relevant, clear component records can support the later evidence review without assigning classifications here.''')],
'''Create separate columns for estimated, approved, paid and credited amounts. Enter quantity and unit cost before adding the actual quoted logistics or service charges. Calculate the total once with each charge identified, then check for double counting. Ask the responsible approver to resolve an unquoted requirement before treating the result as a complete budget. After installation, reconcile the accepted item and financial documents and preserve any outstanding question for the accountant.''',
[('Should blank charges count as zero?','A calculator may total entered values, but an unquoted requirement must remain visibly unresolved. Do not call the entered total complete until the relevant scope and amounts are confirmed.'),('Can this budget predict rental returns?','No. It records cost assumptions and commitments. Revenue depends on other property and market facts, and a furnishing total does not establish an occupancy or pricing uplift.'),('Does the designer decide the tax category?','The designer preserves accurate product and work records. The accountant and relevant study provider determine what further evidence and professional conclusions are required.')],
['Quantity and unit cost','Applicable quoted charges','Included versus additional scope','Allowance and its basis','Payments and credits','Reconciled installed total'],['selection','records','function'],True)
stage('quote-scope-review','Normalize the quote scope','Do the proposals price the same result and assign every necessary task?',[
('Give providers one property brief',
'''A meaningful quote comparison starts with the same intended function, verified constraints and room inventory. Send the accepted brief and identify what remains to be measured or assessed. If one provider receives a different assignment, the headline totals do not answer the same question.

Ask each provider to separate the product or deliverable from the work of ordering, receiving, installing, checking and handing it over where relevant. Record included, excluded and undecided scope. An unmentioned task is a question, not automatic evidence that it is included or unavailable.'''),
('Compare responsibility before price',
'''List the owner for each required task and the evidence that will close it. A consultation, specification, purchasing service and installed result transfer different responsibilities. A lower fee may leave more local coordination with the owner, while a broad service label can still have exclusions.

Review revisions, substitutions, delays, claims and additional visits. Ask for actual terms rather than inferring them from a marketing page. Keep project location, product eligibility and the local team's capacity visible. The right scope depends on the property and the work the owner can support.'''),
('Close the proposal questions in writing',
'''Request an updated scope or written clarification for important gaps. Add actual missing charges to the comparison when quoted, and keep unresolved allowances labeled. Choose from the complete responsibility and cost picture rather than manufacturing a universal winner.

Preserve the accepted proposal, approval and later changes with the purchasing record. Give the receiver, installer and operating team the parts they need. A useful comparison should result in a coherent assignment, not just a spreadsheet declaring which entered number is smaller.''')],
'''Make a task matrix with one row per required action and a column for each provider. Mark the status and responsible person, then attach the exact quote reference. Add a question for every undecided line. Compare full quoted totals only after the scopes align and record the owner work that remains. Keep the final matrix with the accepted agreement so additional charges can be evaluated against an actual responsibility record.''',
[('Does full service mean every task is included?','No. Confirm the written scope for the chosen package and property. Identify products, logistics, trade work and acceptance responsibilities explicitly rather than relying on the label.'),('Is the lowest design fee the lowest project cost?','Not necessarily. Compare the complete result, including quoted goods and implementation responsibilities. Keep unquoted work visible instead of treating it as zero.'),('Should owner labor appear in the comparison?','Describe the work the owner or local team must perform, even when no cash charge is assigned. Capacity and availability can materially change whether the scope is practical.')],
['Provider and dated quote','Common required task','Included excluded or undecided','Named responsibility','Quoted cost or open amount','Accepted clarification'],['function','selection','records'],True)
stage('receiving-and-condition-check','Plan receiving and inspection','How will the team identify and accept the right goods or deliverables?',[
('Prepare before arrival',
'''Give the receiver the exact order or deliverable schedule, approved identifiers, quantities and expected arrival information. Distinguish a shipping estimate from a confirmed appointment. Identify who can make decisions when an incorrect, damaged or incomplete item appears.

Review the seller, carrier and receiving agreement's current inspection and reporting procedures. Record relevant deadlines and the evidence needed for a remedy. Do not invent a universal claim window or assume that a signature establishes complete product acceptance. Keep access, temporary storage and packaging handling in the plan.'''),
('Separate quantities and condition statuses',
'''Use distinct records for ordered, arrived, inspected, accepted and released for installation. Match labels or file identifiers to the approved specification. Preserve relevant photographs and notes according to the provider process. A closed carton or delivered file can still contain a mismatch with the scope.

Separate wrong item, missing quantity, visible damage and an installation concern. Each may need a different response. Keep exception goods or incomplete deliverables distinguishable from accepted inventory so the next person does not use or discard them by mistake.'''),
('Carry exceptions into the schedule',
'''Assign a claim or correction owner and a next review date. Record what the provider has actually agreed to do. A replacement dispatched later is not yet an inspected remedy, and a promised refund is not a reconciled credit. Keep the installation and readiness decision owners informed of effects.

Close the receiving line when the accepted quantity and remedy evidence reconcile. Transfer exact product or deliverable information to installation and the final inventory. The goal is a traceable acceptance process rather than a carton count that hides unresolved work.''')],
'''Choose one order line and walk it from arrival information through identification, inspection and acceptance. Introduce a hypothetical wrong finish, missing component or incomplete deliverable and identify the response from the actual provider procedures. Record who preserves evidence, who requests the remedy and who updates the schedule. Finish by reconciling the accepted result, credits and open items without assuming the correction is complete merely because it was requested.''',
[('Does a delivery signature close the order?','Not by itself. Follow the relevant agreement and provider procedures, and preserve separate records for arrival, condition and accepted quantity.'),('Should packaging be discarded immediately?','Use the actual inspection and claim requirements to decide. Packaging can contain identifiers or evidence needed for an exception, while the project also needs a suitable temporary storage and removal process.'),('What counts as a closed exception?','The accepted remedy or corrected deliverable has been checked and the related quantity, financial and schedule records reconcile. A promise alone is not closure.')],
['Order or deliverable reference','Expected versus received quantity','Identifier and condition evidence','Provider procedure and deadline','Remedy owner and status','Accepted quantity and closure'],['records','failure','measure'])
stage('installation-handoff','Prepare the installation handoff','What does the responsible installer need to deliver the approved result?',[
('Separate assembly from specialist work',
'''Identify the exact tasks required by the approved element. Assembly, mounting, electrical work, plumbing, construction and digital implementation are different assignments. Use product or deliverable instructions and ask the responsible providers to define the appropriate scope. Do not turn a general worksheet into a DIY technical procedure.

Give each task a named owner and acceptance evidence. Confirm who supplies necessary products, hardware and information, and which site conditions must be ready first. An assumed tool or missing document can delay work even when all purchased goods appear to have arrived.'''),
('Provide current site and product records',
'''Send the accepted specification, measurements, instructions and relevant room arrangement together. Identify substitutions and unresolved exceptions clearly. The installer should not have to choose between an old concept board and a new delivery label without an approval record.

Discuss access, temporary storage, packaging and the ordinary operating constraints of the property. Record which decisions require owner approval during the visit. Follow exact manufacturer and qualified professional requirements for technical questions; attractive placement does not establish appropriate installation.'''),
('Define the closeout',
'''List the required final records: installed identifiers, relevant professional evidence, instructions, photographs and open issues. Check the intended guest function and local care access separately from merely observing that an object is in the room.

Record any change from the approved plan and update the inventory and operating guidance. Keep unresolved items on a punch list with an owner and required evidence. A complete handoff describes what was actually delivered and what still needs attention, rather than using a final payment as proof of readiness.''')],
'''Prepare a task-by-task handoff and attach the latest specification and room survey. Ask the responsible installer to identify missing requirements before the scheduled visit. Select one hypothetical substitution and trace how the installer would recognize its approved version. Agree on the photographs or professional documents needed at completion. Finish with a clear process for changes that require owner approval and for open issues that cannot be resolved during the visit.''',
[('Does assembly include mounting or trade work?','Only if the actual agreement says so and the responsible provider can perform the work appropriately. Separate those tasks and confirm requirements before the visit.'),('Which drawing should the installer use?','The accepted current revision, with approved substitutions and exceptions identified. Keep obsolete versions available as history but clearly superseded.'),('What closes an installation task?','The required evidence and relevant function checks have been accepted, or the remaining issue is explicitly assigned. Presence in a room is not the whole acceptance condition.')],
['Required task','Responsible provider','Current product and site documents','Prerequisite or missing requirement','Approval needed during work','Completion evidence'],['selection','measure','records'])
stage('guest-use-commissioning','Commission the guest-use function','Does the installed result perform its stated ordinary task?',[
('Test the real configuration',
'''Prepare the element as guests will receive it, with actual surrounding furnishings, controls and ordinary belongings present. Use the accepted brief to choose the task. A daytime styling review can miss the practical sequence of arrival, use and departure.

Follow exact product instructions and keep technical inspection with the responsible qualified person. Commissioning here means an ordinary-use review of the accepted setup, not a safety or compliance certificate. Record temporary substitutes so a successful observation is not mistakenly applied to a different final product.'''),
("Observe an unfamiliar user’s sequence",
'''Where practical, ask somebody who did not prepare the design to find, operate or use the element under the stated instructions. Record points of confusion, physical interference and missing information separately. A clear label can help an ordinary task, but it is not a remedy for a product defect or technical concern.

Use relevant conditions: occupied furniture, normal lighting, expanded mechanisms or the actual reset configuration. Record the time and setup rather than making a universal claim from one observation. Individual comfort and preferences can differ even when the function is clear.'''),
('Retest the accepted remedy',
'''Give each issue an owner and a specific closure test. Change the layout, instruction or product only after the cause is understood sufficiently to choose an appropriate action. Ask the responsible professional to address technical issues.

Repeat the affected task after the remedy and update room photographs, instructions and inventory. Keep the evidence of what was tested and its limitations. An accepted functional improvement does not prove that it caused later booking, price or review changes.''')],
'''Write the intended task as a short sequence and prepare the actual room or operating setup. Ask the reviewer to follow current guest information without owner coaching where appropriate. Record observations and classify them as instruction, layout, product or specialist-review questions. Choose one remedy and define the retest before implementing it. Close the record only after that check, leaving any other uncertainty assigned rather than calling the whole setup universally suitable.''',
[('Does commissioning replace a professional inspection?','No. It documents ordinary function and clarity in a specific setup. Technical obligations and defects need the appropriate qualified review.'),('Should one guest preference trigger replacement?','Record the feedback and distinguish it from a defect or repeated functional issue. Test a defined question before selecting a major remedy.'),('What if the installed product changes?','Repeat the affected checks and update the record. An observation about the former model or arrangement may no longer apply.')],
['Intended guest task','Actual installed setup','Conditions and reviewer','Observed issue category','Accepted remedy','Retest result and limitation'],['function','test','failure'])
stage('turnover-reset','Build the turnover reset','Can the local team restore the accepted result consistently?',[
('Describe the finished state',
'''Define the arrangement, inventory and condition the local team should restore. Use current installed photographs and exact item identifiers where relevant. The standard should explain the guest function, not only the decorative appearance.

Separate routine resetting from inspection, repair and specialist service. A cleaner should have an exception process for missing, damaged or unclear items rather than being expected to improvise a fix. Record which task requires another provider and who makes the readiness decision when an essential function is affected.'''),
('Walk the full material route',
'''Trace how supplies, textiles, parts or information move through the reset. Include storage, retrieval, removal, permitted cleaning, drying and return where those steps apply. A task can fail because the correct stock is inaccessible even when the selected product is appropriate.

Observe the actual team and property conditions with their knowledge. Distinguish a measured duration from an estimate and keep unusual events visible. One observation does not establish a universal labor benchmark or a guaranteed annual saving.'''),
('Make the standard attainable',
'''Test the accepted process without the owner's explanation. Revise unclear labels, unworkable storage or unnecessary movement. Follow exact product care and professional instructions and do not replace them with a general cleaning tip.

Keep the current reset version obvious and update it after substitutions or room changes. Record recurring exceptions and use them to improve the specification. A useful reset standard survives ordinary operation, rather than existing only as a beautiful installation photograph.''')],
'''Ask the local team to restore the element using its current record. Note the starting condition, supplies and storage locations. Identify one repeated search, movement or instruction problem and classify its cause. Compare a small layout or information change before assuming a new product is needed. Trial the proposed remedy under documented conditions, then update the accepted photographs and exception process if it works.''',
[('Should the reset include every decorative object?','Include what is necessary to restore the agreed function and truthful appearance, using an attainable process. Excess counting can obscure essential inventory and condition issues.'),('Can one faster reset prove savings?','No. Record task scope, team and conditions. Broader financial claims require more comparable evidence and a separate analysis.'),('Who handles a defect found during turnover?','Use the assigned operating process and responsible provider. A reset record should make escalation clear rather than encouraging an improvised repair.')],
['Accepted finished state','Routine task sequence','Supplies and storage','Exception and escalation','Trial observation','Current reset revision'],['reset','test','failure'])
stage('care-and-maintenance','Write the care plan','Can the actual product or deliverable be maintained using supported methods?',[
('Use exact instructions',
'''Collect the care information for the installed model, material, component and revision. Similar appearance does not establish identical care. A removable part is not automatically washable, and a broadly marketed performance label does not define every permitted method.

Identify whether the instructions cover the whole element or only one component. Record missing or contradictory information as a question for the supplier or responsible professional. Do not invent chemical mixtures, structural repairs or technical procedures to fill a gap.'''),
('Match the method to local capacity',
'''Review the actual equipment, handling, drying, storage and specialist support needed. Include the steps before and after the visible cleaning or service task. A supported method can still be impractical for a closely scheduled turnover if the local team cannot complete its full sequence.

Where appropriate and permitted, use a sample or ordinary-use trial to clarify the process. Record conditions and observations without claiming that a short test proves all future performance. Persistent moisture, equipment defects and other technical concerns require appropriate assessment beyond the design care card.'''),
('Keep care linked to replacement',
'''Save product and part identifiers alongside instructions and service contacts. If the method changes, record why and who approved it. Revisit the care plan when a supplier changes the model, material or compatible component.

Track recurring issues and actual remedies. This helps the owner decide whether a different specification, spare or service arrangement would be more practical. Keep the actual invoices and condition history rather than turning an operating observation into an unsupported lifespan or tax conclusion.''')],
'''Build a component-level care card with the exact identifiers and permitted method. Ask the local team to describe equipment, removal, drying and return requirements. Mark an unsupported step as an open question rather than substituting a social-media remedy. Test the approved sequence where practical, then write the exception process for a condition outside routine care. Keep the current card with the installed inventory and any compatible spare stock.''',
[('Does a durable label define the cleaning method?','No. Use the exact instructions for the installed construction and components. Durability marketing is not a complete care process.'),('Can the cleaner use the method from a similar item?','Only after the responsible supplier or professional confirms that it applies. Similar color, material name or shape does not establish compatibility.'),('What should happen when care repeatedly fails?','Record the conditions and obtain appropriate assessment. Review the specification or service process using actual evidence instead of repeating an unsupported treatment.')],
['Installed product and component','Exact care source','Permitted routine method','Local equipment and drying','Exception and specialist contact','Revision and condition history'],['reset','selection','failure'])
stage('spares-and-replacement-support','Plan compatible spares','Which replacement information or stock is useful for this installed element?',[
('Confirm what can be replaced',
'''Identify the components, files or deliverable parts that can be serviced separately. Ask the provider for exact compatible identifiers, revisions and the current ordering or support process. Distinguish a removable piece from a replacement that can actually be obtained.

Record whether information is confirmed or still a question. Current stock does not promise indefinite availability. For a service or information deliverable, preserve the editable source, agreed rights and support responsibility rather than inventing a physical spare part.'''),
('Choose stock from actual need',
'''Compare the effect of a missing component, expected provider lead information, appropriate storage and actual use history. A spare can reduce one kind of interruption while tying up money and space. Avoid adopting a universal quantity or an unsupported product-life assumption.

Label position, orientation and compatibility where those facts matter. Similar-looking parts may not fit the installed revision. Keep suitable storage and authorized service responsibility explicit; having a component on site does not mean every team member can install it appropriately.'''),
('Control withdrawal and obsolescence',
'''Record every use, replacement purchase and transfer. Update the installed inventory when the product changes and remove incompatible stock from active availability. A register should describe what the team can actually find and use, not merely what was once ordered.

Review actual interruption and replacement records periodically. They can improve purchasing and support decisions without establishing a universal failure rate. Preserve receipts and property references for the separate financial review.''')],
'''Select a component whose absence would matter to the stated function. Obtain its exact compatibility information and record the provider response date. Compare buying a spare with maintaining a supported order-and-service process. Include storage and authorized handling in that decision. Ask the local team to locate the recorded stock or source file, then reconcile it with the installed revision. Close the exercise with a reorder or support owner and a trigger for review.''',
[('Does removable mean replaceable?','No. Confirm a compatible component, usable identifier and actual support process. Removal alone does not establish an available remedy.'),('How many spares should be bought?','Use this property’s needs, compatibility, storage and observed history. A generic quantity does not prove that stock will be useful or economical.'),('Can parts be pooled across rentals?','Only with clear compatibility, location and transfer records. Keep installed revisions and property assignments traceable instead of assuming similar appearance means interchangeability.')],
['Installed revision','Replaceable component or source','Compatible identifier','Storage or support location','Authorized user and reorder owner','Use and obsolescence record'],['records','selection','failure'],True)
stage('fault-observation','Triage a condition problem','What is observed, who should assess it, and what evidence closes the issue?',[
('Describe the observation precisely',
'''Record the element, location, date, actual condition and relevant photographs. Distinguish a cosmetic mark, a missing component, unclear operation and a suspected technical defect. Avoid assigning a cause simply because one event occurred before the observation.

Use the operating process to remove an affected item from use or restrict an activity where appropriate, and obtain qualified review for safety, structural, electrical, moisture or other technical concerns. The design worksheet does not diagnose a defect or prescribe an improvised repair.'''),
('Assign the right review',
'''Give the issue to the provider or professional able to assess the actual product and condition. Send identifiers, instructions, installation evidence and the observed history. Keep the original records rather than replacing them with a confident summary unsupported by the evidence.

Separate supplier claims, repair assessment, installation corrections and operating changes. Each can have different responsibilities and financial implications. Record what is known, what remains uncertain and when the next response is due. An unresolved issue should not disappear into a general room-styling note.'''),
('Verify closure and learn from it',
'''Agree on the required evidence for the accepted remedy and recheck the relevant ordinary function. Preserve the condition before and after, actual work performed and any changed product or instruction. A requested remedy is not a completed one.

Use recurring observations to improve specifications, care and spare planning. Keep actual costs and interruption facts for the owner and accountant. Do not turn one incident into a universal failure rate or presume a tax treatment from the issue label.''')],
'''Create one issue record with a photograph, installed identifier and the ordinary task affected. Assign the review owner and note whether a professional assessment is required. Write the evidence that would close the problem before choosing a remedy. After the accepted action, repeat the affected check and update inventory, care or guest information if needed. Keep the earlier evidence so the project history remains understandable.''',
[('Should the owner diagnose the cause from a photo?','A photograph supports an observation but may not establish cause. Obtain the appropriate provider or qualified assessment for the actual issue.'),('Does a cosmetic description make a defect harmless?','No. Record the facts and use the responsible review process. Styling language must not substitute for technical assessment.'),('What makes the issue closed?','The accepted remedy has supporting evidence, relevant function has been rechecked and downstream records reflect any change. A promise or payment alone is not closure.')],
['Observed condition and date','Installed identifier','Affected function','Review owner and technical question','Accepted remedy evidence','Reinspection and record updates'],['failure','test','records'],True)
stage('substitution-approval','Approve a substitution','Does the proposed change preserve the required function and its dependencies?',[
('Start with the reason for the original choice',
'''Record the approved element and why it was selected: function, dimensions, care, compatibility, delivery or agreed deliverable. Identify which requirements are essential and which are preferences. A visually similar alternative can still alter the room or operating process.

Ask for exact information about the replacement before ordering. Keep the original specification and approval visible. Do not treat availability, a lower price or a familiar brand name as proof that the proposed option meets the same assignment.'''),
('Compare the complete change',
'''Put relevant dimensions, construction, care, compatible components, access and service requirements side by side. Review packed and assembled information where delivery matters. For a service or information deliverable, compare scope, format, revision rights and implementation requirements.

Calculate the full cost effect using actual quoted amounts, including relevant logistics, labor, returns or additional visits. Keep unresolved charges labeled. Ask which other elements must change if this one changes; a new footprint can affect a nearby item or the accepted reset arrangement.'''),
('Approve and update the system',
'''Name the approver and preserve the written decision before commitment. Update the purchase, receiver's record, installed inventory, care documents, room layout and guest information as applicable. A substitution is not complete when only the shopping link changes.

Recheck the affected acceptance conditions after installation or delivery. Retain the original and replacement evidence so later repair, spare or financial questions can be answered. The objective is a coherent changed result, not simply a faster order.''')],
'''Use the original requirement as the first column and the proposed replacement evidence as the second. Mark each important line confirmed, different or unresolved. Add the complete quoted cost change and downstream documents affected. Ask the approver to decide from that record. After receiving or implementation, verify the accepted revision and repeat the affected ordinary-use test before closing the change.''',
[('Can a cheaper alternative be approved immediately?','Only after the relevant function, fit, care, scope and full cost are reviewed. A lower product price can create other work or incompatibility.'),('Does the same nominal size prove fit?','No. Review exact assembled, packed and operating details relevant to the property and verify critical constraints.'),('Which documents need updating?','Every record that depends on the changed element, including inventory, care, layout, purchasing and guest information where relevant. Keep the prior decision traceable.')],
['Original requirement and identifier','Proposed evidence','Confirmed difference or gap','Full quoted cost change','Approver and date','Affected documents and retest'],['selection','measure','reset'],True)
stage('phased-refresh','Sequence a refresh','How can this change be coordinated with essential functions and other project work?',[
('Define the usable end of the phase',
'''A phase should end with an accepted function or a clearly described remaining limitation, not merely a set of purchases. Identify what must work before the owner or manager can make the relevant guest-readiness decision. Essential repair, complete furnishing, comfort improvement and optional styling are different priorities.

Record the actual existing condition and what remains in use. Keep specialist assessment separate where required. Do not assume that guests can work around a partially assembled or technically unresolved element just because the project is called a refresh.'''),
('Map dependencies and site access',
'''List what must happen before the change: verification, approval, preparation, trade work, receiving, installation, cleaning or updating information. Group tasks only where the actual providers and property conditions support it. An estimate from another project is not a guaranteed duration here.

Include storage, packaging and movement during the phase. Work stock can consume an area that is normally available to guests or the local team. Ask the operating owner how the changed availability must be handled and keep the photographer or listing manager informed of temporary arrangements.'''),
('Accept the phase and preserve its records',
'''Test the affected functions and record unfinished work with owners and required evidence. Update the installed inventory, instructions and current images. A final styling view does not replace the relevant professional and ordinary-use checks.

Reconcile purchases, credits and actual work descriptions by phase. Preserve the chronology without assigning financial treatment from a label such as cosmetic or upgrade. A well-organized phase makes the next design and accounting conversations clearer while leaving professional conclusions with the responsible advisers.''')],
'''Draw the element's dependency chain to the next required readiness decision. Mark actual provider dates separately from assumptions. Identify a hypothetical delay and record its effect on access, inventory and guest information. Compare a complete limited phase with an incomplete broader one. Finish by specifying the function checks and financial records required before the phase can be called accepted.''',
[('Can purchases alone define a phase?','They can define an order batch, but a usable project phase also needs dependencies, installation or implementation, checks and a clear available result.'),('Should the rental remain open during the change?','The operating owner must decide from actual affected functions and requirements. Do not presume an incomplete setup is acceptable or conceal a changed amenity.'),('Does refresh mean a particular tax treatment?','No. Preserve what was done, purchased and replaced, with dates and evidence. The accountant evaluates treatment from the facts and applicable rules.')],
['Existing condition','Required phase result','Predecessor tasks','Access and temporary availability','Acceptance evidence','Actual work and cost chronology'],['function','failure','records'],True)
stage('photo-and-listing-accuracy','Plan truthful photography','How should the accepted element be represented in current guest information?',[
('Photograph the installed experience',
'''Compare the image plan with actual inventory and readiness. Show the element in the configuration guests will receive and identify meaningful moving or expanded arrangements. Do not stage a temporary feature as if it remains available for the stay.

Use photographs to explain relationships, tasks and limitations as well as appearance. A close-up can be useful but should not be the only evidence for a feature whose access or context matters. Confirm the photographer's agreed scope and the rights required to use the resulting images.'''),
('Keep wording supported by evidence',
'''Describe the actual product or feature using verified information. Avoid broad performance or suitability claims that the image does not establish. An attractive design does not prove increased revenue, universal comfort, accessibility compliance or the quality of a future stay.

If an operating limitation matters before booking, give the listing owner the relevant facts and current images. Ordinary controls or care restrictions may also belong in the guest guide. Keep technical and platform questions with their appropriate sources rather than guessing from a photo.'''),
('Maintain an image and information register',
'''Record selected filenames, dates, room or element identifiers and the channels using them. When a substitution, rearrangement or service change affects the representation, identify which image and text must be updated. Preserve older versions as history but make the current accepted set obvious.

Ask the local team to report discrepancies between the reset standard and published view. The purpose of the register is truthful, maintainable information. A photography task is not finished merely because a folder of images was delivered.''')],
'''Match each proposed image to the actual element and the feature it is intended to explain. Note temporary items, unresolved work and any configuration that needs a second view. Review selected images against the installed inventory and confirm permitted use. Record the publication channels and an update owner. Test whether an unfamiliar reader can understand the feature without inferring something the property does not provide.''',
[('Can a temporary item appear in listing photos?','Its use must not misrepresent what guests will receive. Resolve the actual availability and representation with the listing owner instead of letting a staged image create an unsupported expectation.'),('Does a photograph prove capacity or accessibility?','Not by itself. Use specific supported facts, context and the relevant evidence or professional review where required.'),('When should images be updated?','When meaningful product, layout, availability or feature changes make the prior representation inaccurate. Keep a channel record so updates reach the places that use the images.')],
['Actual installed configuration','Feature or task shown','Temporary or unresolved item','Image filename and rights','Published channels','Update trigger and owner'],['function','test','records'])
stage('warranty-and-provider-support','Review provider support','What remedy, documentation or service responsibility is actually available?',[
('Read the exact terms',
'''Collect the current warranty, service agreement, purchase terms or support scope for the actual element. Identify the provider, product or deliverable revision and relevant use conditions. A headline promise does not describe every exclusion, required record or charge.

Separate manufacturer, retailer, installer and service-provider responsibilities where the documents distinguish them. Coverage can differ for parts, labor, transport or specific components. For an information or custom-design deliverable, confirm revisions, editable files, permitted use and correction responsibilities rather than assuming a retail warranty applies.'''),
('Prepare a usable support record',
'''Keep proof of purchase, installed identifiers, instructions, dates and condition evidence together. Record the current contact and reporting process. Follow the provider's actual requirements instead of inventing a universal deadline or assuming an online review thread is a remedy process.

A warranty or support statement does not establish product suitability for every property. Ask the provider to clarify relevant rental use or scope questions in writing. Keep technical concerns with qualified professionals and do not continue an unsupported operation simply because a claim is pending.'''),
('Reconcile the remedy',
'''Record the requested action, provider response, actual remedy and remaining obligations. Inspect replacements or corrected deliverables against the approved requirement. A dispatched part or promised credit is not yet accepted closure.

Update inventory, care, scope and financial records after the remedy. Preserve costs not covered by the provider separately from hoped-for recovery. This supports later owner and accounting review without declaring a legal entitlement or tax result from a general support summary.''')],
'''Choose one hypothetical support issue and find the exact term or scope provision relevant to it. Assemble the identifiers and evidence the provider requires. Record what is confirmed, excluded or still uncertain and request clarification where necessary. Trace the remedy through actual completion and any financial credit. Finish with the downstream records that must change if the installed element or deliverable is replaced.''',
[('Does a long warranty cover every cost?','Not necessarily. Read the actual terms for components, use, labor, transport and exclusions rather than inferring complete coverage from the headline duration.'),('Is support the same as suitability?','No. A remedy process and the question of whether an element fits this property are separate reviews. Keep product and professional requirements explicit.'),('Should expected recovery reduce the budget now?','Keep a hoped-for recovery distinct from a confirmed and reconciled credit. Record actual costs and provider responses for the responsible financial review.')],
['Exact terms and provider','Covered product or deliverable','Use or scope clarification','Required supporting evidence','Remedy and open obligation','Credit and inventory reconciliation'],['selection','failure','records'],True)
stage('financial-record-handoff','Prepare the financial record handoff','Can purchases, work and installed evidence be traced without invented classifications?',[
('Build a property-specific record',
'''Connect the actual element to its property, room, purchase or work description, invoices, payments and credits. Keep original documents alongside any summary. A shopping board or design label is not enough to explain what was acquired and what services were performed.

Separate acquired inventory, later purchases, installation and subsequent work in the record. Request useful descriptions from vendors when a bundled line is unclear. Preserve the original invoice and the clarification rather than creating an unsupported allocation merely to make a spreadsheet look complete.'''),
('Reconcile changes and chronology',
'''Record accepted quantities, substitutions, returns, transfers and removed items where relevant. Link these events to the current installed inventory and actual dates. Ordered, delivered, installed and ready for intended use are different facts; keep supporting evidence for each applicable event.

A schedule or design handover can help organize the chronology, but it does not by itself establish a tax conclusion. The preparer determines treatment from taxpayer and property facts and applicable rules. A component study, where relevant, has its own evidence and scope requirements.'''),
('Assign professional questions explicitly',
'''List missing documents, unclear descriptions and amounts that do not reconcile. Ask the accountant or study provider which further evidence is required instead of guessing at recovery periods, deductions or allocation percentages. Confirm who owns the property analysis and who implements the return treatment.

Keep the final package versioned with its source documents. The goal is a traceable handoff that supports professional review. It is not an estimate of guaranteed tax savings or a claim that every design cost receives the same classification.''')],
'''Select one installed element and trace its approved order to the invoice, payment, credit and acceptance evidence. Identify any bundled service or later replacement and retain the relevant chronology. Mark an unreconciled amount as a question for the vendor and financial reviewer. Prepare a concise source index so another person can follow the facts without relying on an unsupported design summary.''',
[('Does a furniture package receive one tax treatment?','The design record does not determine that. Preserve product and work detail and let the accountant evaluate the applicable treatment from supported facts.'),('Should missing amounts be allocated by a generic percentage?','Do not manufacture an allocation. Request useful vendor detail and ask the relevant professional what additional evidence or method is appropriate.'),('Does a study replace tax-preparer review?','The property analysis and return implementation have distinct responsibilities. Confirm scope, evidence and handoff with the actual providers and preparer.')],
['Property and installed identifier','Invoice and work description','Payment and credit reconciliation','Actual project chronology','Missing evidence or allocation question','Professional review responsibility'],['records','selection','failure'],True)
stage('operating-review','Review the element after launch','What does actual use show, and which specific change is justified?',[
('Collect observations with context',
'''Keep repeated guest questions, local-team feedback, condition issues and replacement events connected to the actual installed element. Record the date, setup and relevant circumstances. A single preference and a recurring functional problem are different kinds of evidence.

Separate design, product, care, instruction and operating questions. Booking or revenue changes also have pricing, calendar, market and marketing context. A simple before-and-after observation does not prove that a furnishing change caused a commercial outcome.'''),
('Choose a narrow improvement',
'''Define the problem before selecting a remedy. Compare an instruction, layout or care adjustment with repair or replacement where relevant. Include the complete cost, dependencies and appropriate professional review. Keep unresolved technical conditions assigned rather than styling around them.

State what test would show the remedy worked. Use ordinary task evidence and team feedback without pretending a limited trial is a controlled experiment. Record uncertainty and preserve the conditions so a later reviewer can understand the result.'''),
('Update the accepted standard',
'''When a change is accepted, revise the relevant layout, inventory, care plan, reset photograph and guest information. Identify the person responsible for each update. Close the issue only after the actual result is checked, and keep any remaining question visible.

Review the change after further ordinary use. Product revisions, guest tasks or the local team can change the conditions that supported an earlier decision. A useful operating record learns from this property without inventing a universal replacement interval or guaranteed design return.''')],
'''Choose a repeated observation and connect it to the original brief and accepted installation. Classify the issue, propose one defined remedy and list its full effects. Record the evidence needed for a retest and the person who approves the change. After the trial, update the standard only if the result is accepted. Preserve actual costs, limitations and a follow-up trigger rather than extrapolating a commercial forecast.''',
[('Does one complaint prove the design is wrong?','It is evidence to consider, not an automatic diagnosis. Distinguish preference, defect and repeated functional friction, then investigate a specific question.'),('Can revenue changes prove a furnishing return?','Not by themselves. Pricing, availability, seasonality and other factors can also change. Keep the operating design review separate from unsupported causal claims.'),('When should the record be reopened?','When relevant products, layout, use or operating conditions change, or new evidence challenges the accepted result. Preserve the prior version as context.')],
['Observation and context','Issue category','Original requirement','Candidate remedy and full cost','Retest evidence','Accepted updates and review trigger'],['failure','reset','test'])
